WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.259
How many examples?

2
00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:06.083
Less than most people expect to start, and more than most people expect to finish.

3
00:00:06.363 --> 00:00:19.824
OpenAI’s guide requires at least 10 examples and recommends starting with about 50 well-crafted demonstrations, evaluating, and rethinking the task or prompt if 50 shows no effect before adding more.

4
00:00:20.104 --> 00:00:33.693
LIMA got strong results from 1,000 carefully curated examples, which is a statement about quality more than quantity: the authors spent their effort on diversity and consistency, not volume.

5
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:41.717
Provider limits are much higher (Google’s documentation allows millions of examples), but limits are not recommendations.

6
00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:45.880
The defensible way to choose a size is empirical: a learning curve.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:55.291
Train on 50, 100, 200, and 400 examples, score each model on the same validation set, and plot score against size.

8
00:00:55.571 --> 00:00:59.155
If the curve is still rising steeply, labeling more is worth it.

9
00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:08.608
If it has flattened, more of the same data won’t help; you need different data (harder cases, the failure modes from your error analysis) or a different approach.

10
00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:15.288
This also gives you a number your manager will understand: what the next 500 labels are expected to buy.

11
00:01:15.568 --> 00:01:17.317
Coverage matters more than count.

12
00:01:17.597 --> 00:01:34.877
Your examples should span the kinds of input production sees (in the lab: four document types, with and without action items, explicit dates and vague ones, named owners and group requests), in roughly the proportions you expect, with deliberate extra examples of the hard cases.
